 Diabetes Risk Factors: Exploratory Data Analysis

Exploratory analysis of the Pima Indians Diabetes dataset: 768 women aged 21+, eight clinical predictors and a binary diabetes outcome.

[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO/blob/main/Diabetes_EDA.ipynb)

## Questions
1. How does diabetes prevalence vary by age and BMI?
2. Which clinical measures are most strongly associated with diabetes?

## Approach
- Identified and handled **hidden missing values** (zeros recorded for glucose, blood pressure, skin thickness, insulin and BMI)
- Calculated prevalence by BMI category and age group
- Compared glucose distributions by outcome
- Examined correlations with the outcome

## Key findings
- Glucose is the variable most strongly associated with diabetes, followed by BMI, age and pregnancies.
- Blood pressure and skin thickness show weak associations.
- Older age groups have small samples, so their estimates are unstable.

## Limitations
Cross-sectional data from one population (women of Pima heritage), so associations cannot be read as causal or generalised. Median imputation reduces the apparent signal in insulin and skin thickness.

## Run it
```bash
